WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.790
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:11.549
A tangle pattern is a beautiful, intervowen
tapestry of basic stroke patterns, like dots,

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:13.880
straight lines, and simple curves.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.380
If we look at some of these works, we see
that many of these are highly structured,

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.250
and maybe, we could automatically create such
beautiful structures with a computer.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:28.480
And, now, hold on to your papers, because
this piece of work is about generating tangle

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.380
patterns with grammars.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:33.410
Okay, now, stop right there.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:39.430
How on Earth do grammars have anything to
do with computer graphics or tangle patterns?

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:43.610
The idea of this sounds as outlandish as it
gets.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:48.789
Grammars are a set of rules that tell us how
to build up a structure, such as a sentence

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:54.570
properly from small elements, like nouns,
adjectives, pronouns, and so on.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:01:00.840
Math nerds also study grammars extensively
and set up rules that enforce that every mathematical

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.839
expression satisfies a number of desirable
constraints.

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:10.090
It's not a surprise that when mathematicians
talk about grammars, they will use these mathematical

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.729
hieroglyphs like the ones you see on the screen.

00:01:12.729 --> 00:01:17.810
It is a beautiful subfield of mathematics
that I have studied myself before, and am

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:18.810
still hooked.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:25.030
Especially given the fact that from grammars,
we can build not only sentences, but buildings.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:30.580
For instance, a shape grammar for buildings
can describe rules like a wall can contain

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:37.930
several windows, below a window goes a window
sill, one wall may have at most two doors

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.680
attached, and so on.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:45.610
My friend Martin Ilcik is working on defining
such shape grammars for buildings, and using

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:51.329
these grammars, he can generate a huge amount
of different skyscrapers, facades, and all

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:53.340
kinds of cool buildings.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:58.560
In this piece of work, we start out with an
input shape, subdivide it into multiple other

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.550
shapes, assign these smaller shapes into groups.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:07.590
And the final tangle is obtained by choosing
patterns and assigning them to all of these

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:08.590
groups.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:14.190
This yields a very expressive, powerful, tool
that anyone can use to create beautiful tangle

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:20.750
patterns.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:24.070
And all this, through the power of grammars.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.950
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

